.SH NAME
ktm-guide-attributes \- Attributes (chapter 23 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
An attribute is a word starting with \fB@\fR, written before a struct, a field or a parameter. It asks the compiler to write code for the declaration, or tells a library how to treat it. This chapter covers where attributes go, \fB@json\fR and \fB@yaml\fR and the methods they generate, the field attributes \fB@key\fR, \fB@skip\fR and \fB@omitEmpty\fR, the settings attributes \fB@env\fR and \fB@required\fR, the validation rules \fB@min\fR, \fB@max\fR, \fB@email\fR, \fB@url\fR and \fB@oneOf\fR, \fB@caller\fR on parameters, seeing the generated code, and reading attributes through reflection.
.SH Where attributes go
.PP
An attribute is \fB@name\fR or \fB@name(arguments)\fR, before what it applies to. Several can be written one after another:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
@json @yaml
struct User {
    @key("e\-mail") @required String email;
    @skip i64 cacheHits;
};
.EE
.RE
.PP
