.PP
An attribute ktm doesn't know, or one in the wrong place, is a compile error:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
struct User {
    @jsonn u8 *name;
};

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
unknown attribute `@jsonn`
.EE
.RE
.SH @json
.PP
\fB@json\fR before a struct makes the compiler write a JSON encoder and decoder for it, as methods. The struct's file must import \fBjson\fR\&. \fBjson.decode<T>\fR and \fBjson.encode\fR then use them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json, reflect;

@json struct User {
    i64 id;
    String name;
    bool admin;
};

i32 main() {
    User u = json.decode<User>("{\e"id\e": 7, \e"name\e": \e"Ada\e", \e"admin\e": true}");
    printf("%d %s %d\en", u.id, u.name, u.admin);
    u.id = 8;
    scoped String text = json.encode(reflect.of(u));
    printf("%s\en", text);
    u.freeDecoded();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
7 Ada 1
{"id":8,"name":"Ada","admin":true}
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A key the struct has no field for is ignored; a field the JSON leaves out is zero.
.IP \(bu 2
An enum field is written as its member's name.
.IP \(bu 2
Nested structs, slices, \fBvec.Vec<T>\fR and pointers are written and read at any depth.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfreeDecoded()\fR frees what decoding allocated (\fBString\fRs, slices, pointed-to structs); \fBjson.free(reflect.of(u))\fR does the same.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json, reflect;

enum Role { guest, admin };

@json struct Tag {
    String name;
    i64 weight;
};

@json struct User {
    i64 id;
    Role role;
    Tag[] tags;
};

i32 main() {
    User u = json.decode<User>("{\e"id\e": 1, \e"role\e": \e"admin\e", \e"tags\e": [{\e"name\e": \e"math\e", \e"weight\e": 3}], \e"since\e": 1843}");
    printf("%d %s %d %s\en", u.id, reflect.name(u.role), u.tags.len, u.tags[0].name);
    scoped String text = json.encode(reflect.of(u));
    printf("%s\en", text);
    u.freeDecoded();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 admin 1 math
{"id":1,"role":"admin","tags":[{"name":"math","weight":3}]}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without \fB@json\fR the same calls work by reflection, about three times slower to decode; adding \fB@json\fR makes existing code faster with no other change. The struct's file must import \fBjson\fR:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
@json struct User {
    i64 id;
};

i32 main() {
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
needs `import json;`
.EE
.RE
