.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json;

@json struct Point {
    i64 x;
    i64 y;
};

i32 main() {
    Point p = {3, 4};
    json.Writer w = json.writerNew();
    p.toJson(&w);
    scoped String text = w.take();
    w.free();
    printf("%s\en", text);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
{"x":3,"y":4}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A struct with \fB@json\fR and no \fBfree()\fR of its own can be declared \fBscoped\fR: it is freed with \fBfreeDecoded()\fR (see defer and scoped (see ktm-guide-defer-and-scoped(7))).
.SH @yaml
.PP
\fB@yaml\fR does the same for YAML, and the two go together on one struct:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json, yaml;

@json @yaml struct Server {
    String host;
    i64 port;
    String[] aliases;
};

i32 main() {
    scoped Server s = json.decode<Server>("{\e"host\e": \e"example.com\e", \e"port\e": 443, \e"aliases\e": [\e"www\e", \e"api\e"]}");
    yaml.Writer y = yaml.writerNew();
    s.toYaml(&y);
    scoped String doc = y.take();
    y.free();
    printf("%s", doc);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
host: example.com
port: 443
aliases:
  \- www
  \- api
.EE
.RE
.SH Field attributes for JSON and YAML
.PP
\fB@key("name")\fR is the field's key in the text, \fB@skip\fR leaves the field out entirely, and \fB@omitEmpty\fR leaves it out of the output when it is 0, empty or null:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json;

@json struct User {
    i64 id;
    @key("e\-mail") String email;
    @omitEmpty String nickname;
    @skip i64 cacheHits;
};

i32 main() {
    scoped User u = json.decode<User>("{\e"id\e": 7, \e"e\-mail\e": \e"ada@example.com\e"}");
    u.cacheHits = 99;
    json.Writer w = json.writerNew();
    u.toJson(&w);
    scoped String text = w.take();
    w.free();
    printf("%s\en", text);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
{"id":7,"e\-mail":"ada@example.com"}
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBnickname\fR is empty, so \fB@omitEmpty\fR left it out; \fBcacheHits\fR is \fB@skip\fR, so it is never written, whatever its value.
.PP
Strict decoding refuses keys the struct doesn't have and keys it misses, but a \fB@skip\fR field is never required:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json;

@json struct Point {
    i64 x;
    i64 y;
    @skip i64 cached;
};

i32 main() {
    u8[] inputs[3] = {"{\e"x\e": 1, \e"y\e": 2}", "{\e"x\e": 1}", "{\e"x\e": 1, \e"y\e": 2, \e"z\e": 3}"};
    for (i64 i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        try {
            Point p = json.decode<Point>(inputs[i], json.STRICT);
            printf("ok: %d %d\en", p.x, p.y);
        } catch (Error e) {
            printf("%s\en", e.message);
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
ok: 1 2
1:9: y: missing
1:22: z: no such field in Point
.EE
.RE
.PP
A field whose type can't be written (a union, a function pointer, a struct without \fB@json\fR when the others need it) is an error at that field; mark it \fB@skip\fR\&.
.SH Settings: @env and @required
.PP
\fBconfig.fromEnv\fR fills a struct of settings from environment variables. A field's variable is a prefix and its name in \fBSNAKE_CASE\fR (\fBdatabaseUrl\fR with \fBAPP_\fR reads \fBAPP_DATABASE_URL\fR). \fB@env("NAME")\fR names the variable exactly, and \fB@required\fR makes a missing one an error:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import config, proc, reflect;

struct Settings {
    i64 port;
    @required String databaseUrl;
    @env("LOG_LEVEL") u8[] level;
};

i32 main() {
    proc.setenv("APP_DATABASE_URL", "postgres://localhost/app");
    proc.setenv("LOG_LEVEL", "warn");
    Settings s;
    s.port = 8080;
    config.fromEnv(reflect.of(s), "APP_");
    printf("%d %s %.*s\en", s.port, s.databaseUrl, s.level.len, s.level.ptr);
    config.free(reflect.of(s));
    proc.unsetenv("APP_DATABASE_URL");
    try {
        config.fromEnv(reflect.of(s), "APP_");
    } catch (Error e) {
        printf("%s\en", e.message);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
8080 postgres://localhost/app warn
config: APP_DATABASE_URL is required
.EE
.RE
.PP
A field keeps what it holds when its variable isn't set, so defaults are whatever the struct holds first (\fBport\fR above). See config (see ktm-config(3)).
.SH Validation rules
.PP
\fBvalidate.check\fR checks a struct's values against rules written on its fields, and collects one error per broken rule, naming the field:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect, str, validate;

struct Signup {
    @required @email u8[] email;
    @required @min(3) @max(20) String name;
    @min(18) i64 age;
    @oneOf("free", "pro") u8[] plan;
};

i32 main() {
    Signup s;
    s.email = "ann@example";
    s.name = str.from("an");
    s.age = 17;
    s.plan = "gold";
    validate.Errors errs;
    if (!validate.check(reflect.of(s), &errs)) {
        for (i64 i = 0; i < errs.items.len; i++) {
            validate.FieldError e = errs.items.get(i);
            printf("%s: %s\en", e.field, e.message);
        }
    }
    errs.free();
    s.name.free();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
email: not an email address
name: must have at least 3 characters
age: must be at least 18
plan: must be one of free, pro
.EE
.RE
.PP
