.IP \(bu 2
An empty text or slice that isn't \fB@required\fR passes its other rules; a number is always checked.
.IP \(bu 2
Nested structs, pointed-to structs and slices of structs are checked too.
.IP \(bu 2
The rules work on any struct, with or without \fB@json\fR; \fB@skip\fR fields are left alone.
.IP \(bu 2
A rule on a field it can't check (\fB@email\fR on an \fBi64\fR) makes \fBcheck\fR throw \fBvalidate.ERR_VALIDATE\fR\&.
.PP
See validate (see ktm-validate(3)) for the error list's methods and HTTP use.
.SH @caller on parameters
.PP
A parameter marked \fB@caller("file")\fR, \fB@caller("line")\fR or \fB@caller("func")\fR is filled in by every call with where that call is written. Callers never pass it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void trace(@caller("file") u8 *file, @caller("line") i64 line, @caller("func") u8 *func, u8 *what) {
    printf("%s:%d in %s: %s\en", file, line, func, what);
}

void load() {
    trace("loading");
}

i32 main() {
    trace("start");
    load();
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
example.ktm:10 in main: start
example.ktm:6 in load: loading
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfile\fR is the source file's path as ktm was given it, \fBfunc\fR the function the call is in; both are \fBu8 *\fR\&. \fBline\fR is an integer.
.IP \(bu 2
They can go anywhere in the parameter list, and have no default.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors (see ktm-guide-errors(7)) uses them to report where a check failed.
.SH Seeing the generated code
.PP
\fBktm \-\-expand\fR prints the methods \fB@json\fR and \fB@yaml\fR write, on stderr:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ ktm \-\-expand user.ktm \-o user
.EE
.RE
.SH Attributes through reflection
.PP
The type information of a struct carries its attributes: whether it is \fB@json\fR, and each field's key and \fB@skip\fR (see Reflection (see ktm-guide-reflection(7))):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import json, reflect;

@json struct Item {
    @key("item\-id") i64 id;
    @skip i64 internal;
    u8 *label;
};

i32 main() {
    reflect.Type *t = reflect.typeOf(Item);
    printf("json: %d\en", t.json);
    for (i64 i = 0; i < t.fields.len; i++) {
        reflect.Field f = t.fields[i];
        printf("%.*s key=%.*s skip=%d\en", f.name.len, f.name.ptr, f.key.len, f.key.ptr, f.skip);
    }
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
json: 1
id key=item\-id skip=0
internal key=internal skip=1
label key=label skip=0
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBField.key\fR is the key a field is written under: its \fB@key\fR, or its name.
.SH Rules
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@json\fR and \fB@yaml\fR go before the struct's definition (and before \fBpub\fR), not before a declaration ahead; the file imports \fBjson\fR or \fByaml\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Field attributes go before the field's type.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@key\fR, \fB@skip\fR, \fB@omitEmpty\fR shape JSON and YAML; \fB@env\fR, \fB@required\fR are read by \fBconfig\fR; \fB@required\fR, \fB@min\fR, \fB@max\fR, \fB@email\fR, \fB@url\fR, \fB@oneOf\fR by \fBvalidate\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB@caller\fR goes on parameters only.
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown or misplaced attribute is a compile error.
